00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.322
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.490
When it comes to GKE, there are
two distinct types or levels

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.400
of authorization that you
should be familiar with--

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.220
Google Cloud IAM and
Kubernetes Role-Based

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.590
Access Control, or RBAC.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:50.450
The IAM in Google Cloud IAM
stands for Identity and Access

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.290
Management.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.240
Basically, IAM
tools are what you

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:59.180
use to set up rules for who
can do what to which resources.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.760
The who in identity
and access management

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.099
is going to be some
form of account,

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:08.270
and IAM allows you to set limits
on what that account can do

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.320
and with which resources
in Google Cloud.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.360
You can design your
own custom IAM roles.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.460
But, for GKE, there are also
several predefined Cloud IAM

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.380
roles to quickly and
relatively easily

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:26.780
provide granular access to
Kubernetes Engine resources.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.560
The GKE Viewer role
gives read-only access,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.500
as might be needed for auditing.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.830
The GKE Developer role is
suited to developers and release

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:37.730
engineers.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:42.260
It grants full control to all
resources within a cluster.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.870
The GKE Admin role
gives full access

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.000
to clusters and the
Kubernetes Engine

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.220
resources inside the clusters.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.670
Example users are project
owners, system administrators,

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.830
and on-call engineers.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:59.450
The GKE Cluster Admin role is
used to create, delete, update,

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:03.260
and view clusters, but provides
no access to Kubernetes

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.430
resources within the cluster.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.419
You can also create a custom
role in Google Cloud IAM

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:11.690
to create a role with a more
minimal set of permissions

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.920
than those that are predefined.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.750
So Google Cloud IAM can be used
to control Google Cloud users'

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:22.160
access to the GKE cluster
and its related resources.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.190
For managing access to
resources within the cluster,

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.800
Kubernetes has its
own similar concept--

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.520
Role-Based Access
Controls, or RBAC.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.770
Separate accounts are
needed within the cluster

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.200
to control cluster
users' access.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.350
Just like with Cloud
IAM, RBAC is the tool

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.870
for configuring who
can do what with which

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.880
resources within the cluster.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.900
So Cloud IAM lets you manage
who can do what to which

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.380
resources at the Cloud
level, controlling access

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.370
to the Google Cloud project, GKE
cluster, and other resources,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.650
like Cloud Storage.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.830
Kubernetes Role-Based
Access Control, or RBAC,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.720
manages who can
do what with which

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.850
resources within the cluster
on Kubernetes resources

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:11.960
like deployments, persistent
volumes, et cetera.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.450
But what if the thing you want
to give permissions to you

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.620
isn't a user, but a workload
running within Kubernetes?

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.580
For cases where the who is a
workload, both Google Cloud

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.700
IAM and Kubernetes
RBAC have a concept

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.800
called a service account.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.770
At this point, it can
get a bit confusing.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.320
There's something called
a service account at both

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.170
the Cloud and Kubernetes level.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.870
A Kubernetes service account
is part of your Kubernetes

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.660
cluster, and it can be used
to give a workload running

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.480
within the cluster access to
other parts of the cluster.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.440
Its scope is contained within
the Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.620
A Google Cloud service account
is part of your Google Cloud

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:52.580
project.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.220
The who of a Google
Cloud service account

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.540
can be a Google Cloud resource,
like a GKE cluster, which

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.570
can be given permissions via
IAM to access other Cloud

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.350
resources.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.530
So what if you have
a situation where

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.650
a workload running
within GKE needs access

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.185
to some other Google Cloud
resource, like BigQuery?

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.660
To get permissions all
the way from a workload

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.180
within Kubernetes to
other resources running

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.110
in Google Cloud in the
most granular way possible,

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.590
another concept is needed to
tie the two service account

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.300
concepts together.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.210
That concept is
Workload Identity.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.060
Workload Identity can be
used to automatically connect

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.030
a Google Cloud service account
to a Kubernetes service account

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.390
so that any workloads
represented by the Kubernetes

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:44.840
service account will have the
access described by the GCP

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.840
service account in order
to use any Google Cloud

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.010
APIs, products, or services
that workload might need.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:55.040
Workload Identity
provides granular control,

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.630
offering just the
right permissions

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.360
to a particular
workload rather than to,

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.630
for example, a whole compute
node or a whole cluster,

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:04.370
which may have multiple
workloads running

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:08.180
on them that don't need
the same level of access.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.070
You would configure
Workload Identity

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.710
using something similar to
the two commands shown here.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.760
The first command at the
top uses the gcloud tool

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.530
to make a call to the GCP APIs
to create a policy binding,

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.330
granting the Kubernetes
service account

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.010
access to act as the associated
Google Cloud service account.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:29.840
The second command,
at the bottom,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.200
uses the kubectl tool to use
Kubernetes APIs to annotate

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.140
the Kubernetes service account
with the name of the Google

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.320
Cloud service account
it will act as.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.200
These concepts can be a
little confusing at first,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.180
but Workload Identity
can help make

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.910
access management for workloads
running within your GKE

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.260
clusters much smoother
and easier to manage.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.020
In this episode, we talked
about managing access

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.440
to resources at the
Cloud and cluster levels

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.600
and about simplifying
access management

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:04.830
for Kubernetes workloads via
Google Cloud Workload Identity.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.880
With the concepts
discussed in this video,

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.520
we're confident
you'll be on your way

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.680
to building a GKE cluster
that suits your needs.

